WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:14.946
Howdy folks, I'm Elmer. I've been a ham for over forty years, and I want to tell you the one rule that changed everything for me. I call it the twenty-five percent rule, and it's simple: never pay more than seventy-five percent of a radio's fair market value.

00:00:14.946 --> 00:00:27.402
Now, fair market value isn't what somebody's asking. An asking price is an opinion. A sold price is a fact. You take what radios actually sold for, line 'em up and take the one in the middle. That's your number.

00:00:27.402 --> 00:00:49.199
Here's why twenty-five percent matters. Say a radio's worth a thousand dollars, and you buy it for seven-fifty. If you ever sell it, the selling fees will take about a hundred and fifty. That leaves a hundred dollars of profit in your pocket, and you got to enjoy the radio the whole time you owned it. Buy right, and a radio stops being an expense and starts being an investment.

00:00:49.199 --> 00:01:04.457
I learned this the hard way. Back in two thousand eight, times got tough for my family, and my radios helped carry us through, because I'd always bought right. My wife never argued about another radio coming through the door, because she knew: I buy right, I never lose.

00:01:04.457 --> 00:01:11.930
So that's the rule. Twenty-five percent below fair market. It turns this hobby into something that pays you back. Stick around. Seventy-threes, my friends.
